    "result_text": "The full suite is green (36 tests, 0 failures). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented country-code-specific national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `ValidateOwnerFields.java`.\n\n**Change:** In `toE164(...)`, after the general E.164 8\u201315 digit bound, I added a call to a new `hasValidNationalLength(digits)` helper. It inspects the country code prefix of the assembled digit string:\n\n- `+61` (prefix `61`) \u2192 requires exactly **9** national digits\n- `+1` (prefix `1`) \u2192 requires exactly **10** national digits\n- Any other country code \u2192 no extra rule; only the general E.164 bound applies\n\nWhen the national-number length is wrong for the country, `toE164` returns `null`, which makes `normalizeTelephone` add a `telephone` error \u2014 the escalation handler turns that into a **400**, exactly as the spec requires.\n\nThe prefixes are disjoint (`61\u2026` starts with `6`, never `1`), so the checks are unambiguous. Existing behavior is preserved: `+61412345678` (9 national digits) and `+6421123456` (a `+64` number with no rule) still pass, while the new `+61 123` case is rejected.\n\nAll tests pass, including the new `Cp26Tests` and the previously existing telephone tests (Cp02, Cp03, Cp08) \u2014 36 tests, `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
